    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the confusion earlier! I was just trying to keep my thoughts in the right order, but it seems I got a bit too distracted by the goblin analogy and forgot to finish the explanation you needed for your exam.\n\nLet's get back to the physics. **Bernoulli's Principle** states that in a moving fluid, an increase in speed occurs simultaneously with a decrease in pressure.\n\nHere is how it applies to the wing:\n1.  **Shape:** The wing is curved on top and flatter on the bottom. This shape forces the air traveling over the top to cover a longer distance than the air traveling underneath in the same amount of time.\n2.  **Speed:** Because the air on top has to move faster to meet up with the air on the bottom (assuming they start at the same time), the air speed over the top surface increases.\n3.  **Pressure:** According to Bernoulli, as that air speeds up over the top, its pressure drops. Meanwhile, the air moving slower underneath the wing maintains higher pressure.\n4.  **Result:** This creates a pressure difference where the high-pressure air below pushes up against the low-pressure air above, generating lift.",
